---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { version, release, image, repository, docHref } from '../../lib/site';
const download = (platform: string) =>
  `${repository}/releases/download/v${version}/curlimp-${version}-${platform}.tar.gz`;
---

<Doc
  title="Installation and downloads"
  slug="installation"
  section="Getting started"
  description="Install Curlimp with Homebrew, Docker, or native downloads for Linux amd64, Linux arm64 and experimental macOS Apple Silicon. Upgrade and uninstall safely."
>
  <h2 id="homebrew">Homebrew</h2>
  <p>
    With <a href="https://brew.sh/">Homebrew</a> installed, use the{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/JuanFontes/homebrew-tap">
      project-maintained tap
    </a>{' '}
    on macOS Apple Silicon or Linux amd64/arm64:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`brew install juanfontes/tap/curlimp\ncurlimp --version\ncurlimp -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200`}
  />
  <p>
    This command adds the tap and downloads the corresponding native release,
    with its SHA-256 checksum checked by Homebrew. It keeps the bundled
    libraries together and installs Zsh profile completion. No compiler or
    separate tap command is needed. macOS support remains experimental, requires
    macOS 13 or later, and is tested on macOS 15. Intel Macs and Windows are not
    supported by this formula.
  </p>
  <p>
    Homebrew does not edit your shell configuration or register completions in a
    terminal tab that is already open. If your Zsh setup loads Homebrew
    completions, open a new tab after installation. To keep using the current
    tab, register Curlimp with{' '}
    <code>source &lt;(curlimp --completions zsh)</code>
    after completion has been initialized. For the full setup and selection
    menu, follow the{' '}
    <a href={`${docHref('completion')}#homebrew-installations`}>
      Homebrew completion instructions
    </a>
    .
  </p>
  <h3>Upgrade a Homebrew installation</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={`brew update\nbrew upgrade curlimp`} />
  <p>
    The tap checks for new stable releases hourly and updates only after its
    package tests pass. Scheduled jobs may be delayed; updates are not
    immediate.
  </p>
  <h3>Uninstall a Homebrew installation</h3>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`brew uninstall curlimp\n\n# Optional: remove the package source too.\nbrew untap juanfontes/tap`}
  />
  <p>
    Uninstall removes the package, command link, and managed completion file. It
    leaves your saved responses, shell configuration, and Homebrew download
    cache alone. Installation and removal are tested on all three supported
    architectures in the tap's CI.
  </p>
  <h2>Run the published Docker image</h2>
  <p>
    No compiler or repository checkout is required. Docker selects the matching
    Linux amd64 or arm64 image.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={`docker run --rm ${image} --version`} />
  <p>
    No tag is needed for everyday use. Docker defaults to <code>latest</code>,
    which follows the newest stable release. Versioned tags stay tied to their
    release; choose a version or digest for a fixed dependency. See{' '}
    <a href={`${docHref('docker')}#updates-and-fixed-versions`}>
      updates and fixed versions
    </a>{' '}
    for examples and Docker's local-cache behavior.
  </p>
  <h2>Download a native bundle</h2>
  <p>
    Native downloads run without Docker. Download the archive for your host and{' '}
    <a href={`${repository}/releases/download/v${version}/SHA256SUMS`}>
      SHA256SUMS
    </a>{' '}
    from the <a href={release}>same release</a>.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Platform</th>
        <th>Download</th>
        <th>Status</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Linux x86-64</td>
        <td>
          <a href={download('linux-amd64')}>linux-amd64.tar.gz</a>
        </td>
        <td>Ubuntu 24.04 CI</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Linux ARM64</td>
        <td>
          <a href={download('linux-arm64')}>linux-arm64.tar.gz</a>
        </td>
        <td>Ubuntu 24.04 ARM CI</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>macOS Apple Silicon</td>
        <td>
          <a href={download('macos-arm64')}>macos-arm64.tar.gz</a>
        </td>
        <td>Experimental; macOS 15 CI</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>macOS Intel / Windows</td>
        <td>No native download</td>
        <td>Use a suitable Docker installation</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Linux</h3>
  <p>
    In the directory containing the archive and checksum file, set{' '}
    <code>arch=arm64</code> for ARM Linux or <code>arch=amd64</code> for x86-64:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`set -e\narch=amd64\nbundle="curlimp-${version}-linux-$arch"\nawk -v file="$bundle.tar.gz" '$2 == file' SHA256SUMS | sha256sum --check\ntar -xzf "$bundle.tar.gz"\n"./$bundle/curlimp" --version\n"./$bundle/curlimp" -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200`}
  />
  <p>
    Keep the entire extracted directory together. The launcher uses its bundled
    loader, libraries and CA certificates. Copying only the inner executable is
    not sufficient. The bundle needs <code>/bin/sh</code> and standard utilities
    including <code>readlink -f</code>; it is not a universal static Linux
    binary.
  </p>
  <h3>macOS Apple Silicon</h3>
  <CodeBlock
    code={`set -e\nbundle=curlimp-${version}-macos-arm64\nawk -v file="$bundle.tar.gz" '$2 == file' SHA256SUMS | shasum -a 256 --check\ntar -xzf "$bundle.tar.gz"\n"./$bundle/curlimp" --version\n"./$bundle/curlimp" -sS -I https://httpbin.org/status/200`}
  />
  <p>
    The executable needs the engine in the adjacent <code>lib/</code> directory.
    Keep them together. No Homebrew installation is required at runtime.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      The macOS package is signed ad hoc, not with Developer ID, and is not
      notarized. The deployment target is macOS 13.0, but CI validates macOS 15.
      Older releases and the downloaded-file Gatekeeper flow are not covered by
      CI. Follow{' '}
      <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102445">
        Apple's per-app approval instructions
      </a>{' '}
      if macOS offers Open Anyway after you verify and trust the download.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <h2>Understand verification and trust</h2>
  <p>
    Checksums detect corruption. The checksum manifest is not cryptographically
    signed. Linux uses the bundled certificate file by default; macOS has Apple
    SecTrust support and uses <code>/etc/ssl/cert.pem</code>.{' '}
    <a href={docHref('networking')}>Custom trust options</a> remain available.
  </p>
  <h2>Put the native command on your PATH</h2>
  <p>
    Homebrew links the command automatically. For a manually downloaded bundle,
    move the complete extracted directory to a permanent location and symlink
    its launcher into a directory on your <code>PATH</code>. Do not separate the
    executable from its libraries. The guides use this native
    <code>curlimp</code> command directly.
  </p>
  <h2>Enable Zsh autocomplete</h2>
  <p>
    The native command can supply browser profile suggestions for any prefix.
    Follow the <a href={docHref('completion')}>Zsh setup guide</a> to enable Tab
    completion and menu selection. This setup uses the native executable on your
    host; running a Docker container does not configure your shell.
  </p>
  <h2>Upgrade</h2>
  <p>
    For native downloads, download and verify the new complete bundle. Updating
    it also refreshes its bundled dependencies and certificates. For a fixed
    Docker version, change the tag in your command when ready to upgrade. If you
    use <code>{image}</code> without a tag, refresh the locally cached image
    with:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={`docker pull ${image}`} />
  <p>
    Use Docker's <code>--pull=always</code> when you want each invocation to
    check the registry. See the{' '}
    <a href={`${docHref('docker')}#updates-and-fixed-versions`}>Docker guide</a>{' '}
    for complete commands.
  </p>
</Doc>
